--TEST--
Core\Arr::unique compares by strict identity where array_unique compares by SORT_STRING spelling, so a mixed-type subject keeps entries PHP collapses
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`array_unique`'s default flag is `SORT_STRING`: it casts every element to a string and drops an entry whose *spelling* has been seen, so `0`, `"0"`, `false`, `null` and `""` are two entries rather than five. `Core\Arr::unique` asks the one strict-identity question `nvs_runtime::identity` defines and every set-shaped member shares — `contains`, `diff`, `intersect`, `ObjectSet` — so an `int` and its decimal spelling are two values here exactly as they are there. Reproducing PHP's rule would make this the single `Core` member whose answer depends on how a value is written rather than on what it is, and would make it disagree with the four members it is supposed to agree with; `nvs_core_arr_unique`'s doc comment owns that reasoning. The agreeing half — every subject whose values are one type — is asserted against PHP itself in `arr-unique-matches-array_unique-over-one-typed-list`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Render {
    public static function pairs(array<int|string> $a): string {
        var $out = "";
        foreach ($a as string $k => int|string $v) {
            $out = $out . $k . "=" . $v . ";";
        }
        return $out;
    }

    public static function keys(array<mixed> $a): string {
        var $out = "";
        foreach ($a as string $k => mixed $v) {
            $out = $out . $k . ",";
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

// The narrowest form of the divergence. `array_unique($spellings)` answers
// `0=1;2=2;`, having dropped each string because an int of the same spelling
// came first; Novis keeps all four, because 1 and "1" are two values.
//
// Note that the rendering cannot tell them apart — `echo` writes both as `1` —
// so it is the surviving *keys* that carry the claim here, as they do below.
array<int|string> $spellings = [1, "1", 2, "2"];
echo Render::pairs(Core\Arr::unique($spellings)), "\n";

// The four values `SORT_STRING` folds onto the empty string, plus the pair it
// folds onto "0". `array_unique($wide)` answers two entries at keys 0 and 2 —
// the `0` and the `false` — where Novis keeps every one of the five, none of
// which is identical to any other.
array<int|string|bool|null> $wide = [0, "0", false, null, ""];
var $kept = Core\Arr::unique($wide);
echo Core\Arr::count($kept), " ", Render::keys($kept), "\n";

// The half that does not diverge, on a subject one type wide: the two rules
// coincide, so this row is the same in both languages and is asserted against
// PHP in the matching case named above.
array<int> $list = [1, 2, 1];
echo Render::pairs(Core\Arr::unique($list)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
0=1;1=1;2=2;3=2;
5 0,1,2,3,4,
0=1;1=2;
